Orchestrator Infrastructure Control

Status: Accepted

Context:
- Managing the Orchestrator's static & dynamic resources manually 
  or via the Go code will become cumbersome.


Decision:
- Orchestrator will use a local system folder to hold a Git repository.
  This repo will hold the infrastructure-as-yaml files.
  /orchestrator-root
    /bootstrap
      - root-application.yaml  // "App of Apps" pointing to infra
      - appset-factory.yaml    // ApplicationSet for Projects (dynamic)
    /infra
      - kustomization.yaml     // Bundles core infra components
      /rbac
      /service-accounts
      /prometheus
      /grafana
      /vLLM
      /dcgm
      etc...
    /tenants                          // Programmatically generated by Orchestrator
      - kustomization.yaml            // Aggregates all user projects
      - project-example-project.yaml  // Generated AppProject manifest
      etc...

- ArgoCD will be used to watch the infra/ and tenants/ paths and detect changes
  to spin up new project information
- ApplicationSet acts as a factory implementation for Application manifests
  to avoid Kubernetes API calls for resource creation.

Consequences: 
- Orchestrator's system information becomes bound by local folder structure
  - Could be mitigated in the future easily with a reliable repository
- Risk of hitting Git rate limits as project count grows
  - Might be able to use a webhook endpoint instead of aggressive polling
- ApplicationSet factory increases blast-radius in the event of misconfiguration
- A misconfiguration in a manifest repo may lead to instant deletion of resources
  - Avoid prune for now, rely on orchestrator manually cleanning.

- Orchestrator must implement a feedback loop to verify ArgoCD Status
  - When creating a new project, we must check if the deployment was actually successful
- No flexibility in manifest repo structure

- State of the cluster is derived from the 'dual source of truth' of the Git 
  repo and the Kubernetes cluster state.
- Builds on Orchestrator Reconstruction ADR through state 're-discovery'


